# Website Redirection Script (Educational Example)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Legitimate Website</title>
    <script>
        // This is a demonstration of how malicious redirection could work
        // In a real attack, this would be injected via XSS or server compromise

        // Method 1: Immediate redirect
        // window.location.href = "https://malicious-site.com";

        // Method 2: Delayed redirect (more stealthy)
        setTimeout(function() {
            window.location.href = "https://malicious-site.com";
        }, 3000); // Redirects after 3 seconds

        // Method 3: Using meta refresh (less common in modern attacks)
        // document.write('<meta http-equiv="refresh" content="3;url=https://malicious-site.com">');

        // Method 4: Using iframe overlay (can be more deceptive)
        // document.body.innerHTML = '<iframe src="https://malicious-site.com" width="100%" height="100%"></iframe>';
    </script>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Welcome to our legitimate website</h1>
    <p>This is a demonstration of how malicious redirection could occur.</p>
</body>
</html>
```

## How This Could Be Exploited in the Real World

1. **Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)**: An attacker could inject this script into a vulnerable website through an XSS vulnerability.
2. **Server Compromise**: If an attacker gains access to a website's server, they could modify files to include this redirection.
3. **Phishing**: The script could be part of a phishing page designed to look like a legitimate site.

## Defensive Measures

To protect against such attacks:
1. Implement Content Security Policy (CSP) headers
2. Sanitize all user input to prevent XSS
3. Keep all software updated and patched
4. Use HTTPS to prevent man-in-the-middle attacks
5. Monitor for unauthorized changes to your website files

